description:
FILED s543l (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27), from the reviewing session's L4 (review of s543l, probes-1127/rl/p/l-10.pl row 7); base = tree (MAIN-READY-110 and the s543l tree answer the same).

4-arg `select RBITS, WBITS, EBITS, TIMEOUT` WITH descriptor sets returns -1 with $! = EINTR the moment a CHILD of the program exits: SBCL keeps its own SIGCHLD handler installed (run-program's bookkeeping, not-supported ns-sig "$SIG{CHLD} = 'IGNORE' does not auto-reap"), so the kernel interrupts select(2) with EINTR, and %p-select-4arg hands the -1 straight back.  perl has no CHLD handler unless the program installs one (SIG_DFL), so the kernel does not interrupt the call: select keeps waiting until a descriptor is ready or the timeout ends.

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 | ./runpcl):
    use Socket; socketpair(my $s1, my $s2, AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0) or die;
    my $rin = ""; vec($rin, fileno($s1), 1) = 1;
    my $pid = fork; if (!$pid) { select(undef, undef, undef, 0.3); exit 0 }
    my $t0 = time; my $n = select(my $rout = $rin, undef, undef, 2);
    print "n=$n eintr=", ($!{EINTR} ? 1 : 0), " dt=", time - $t0, "\n";
  perl: n=0 eintr=0 dt=2       PCL: n=-1 eintr=1 dt=0
  The same with a `open my $p, "-|", "sleep 0.3; echo y"` child.  The undef-sets form (`select(undef, undef, undef, T)`, a sleep) is right: it is restarted.

WHAT p-select's 4-arg form does with RBITS today: %p-select-4arg builds real fd_set buffers from the masks (perl's vec() layout), calls select(2) ONCE through alien-funcall with the timeval, and on n < 0 saves errno and returns -1 -- no restart on EINTR, no check whether the signal that interrupted it was one the PROGRAM handles.  not-supported.md has no entry for it.

DIRECTION (not built): on EINTR, when no Perl handler ran during the call (a signal only SBCL handles: CHLD with $SIG{CHLD} unset, or one the relay forwarded and the program does not own), restart select(2) with the REMAINING time (Linux updates the timeval; elsewhere recompute it from a monotonic clock); return -1/EINTR only when a Perl handler ran (perl's own case: a handler that returns interrupts select with EINTR).  The review's l-10 row 7 (an alarm handler that dies during a 4-arg select returned at once, after=7) is this task: the `-|` child of its row 6 exits during row 7's select.  Probe IO::Select::can_read (core) beside it: it returns the empty list on -1.

WHY IT MATTERS: IO::Select / 4-arg select loops over a child's pipes stop early when the child exits -- IPC::Cmd::_open3_run (core) loops `while (my @ready = $selector->can_read)`, so a -1 ends the loop before EOF is read; everyday/modules/IO-Select-Socket.pl selects beside a forked child.
